| Naproxen sodium tablets | Glenmark | 17231956 | Ongoing | [D-0346-2025](https://api.fda.gov/drug/enforcement.json?search=recall_number%3A%22D-0346-2025%22) |
| Naproxen tablets | Glenmark | 19231903, 19231858, 19231881, 19233484, 19233490 | Ongoing | [D-0124-2024](https://api.fda.gov/drug/enforcement.json?search=recall_number%3A%22D-0124-2024%22) |
| Other historical naproxen products and active ingredients | Various firms | Various specified or all-lot ranges | Ongoing or terminated | [FDA search results](https://api.fda.gov/drug/enforcement.json?search=product_description%3A%22naproxen%22&sort=report_date%3Adesc&limit=20) |

**What still needs verification**

A pharmacist, dispensing pharmacy, or manufacturer should compare the actual bottle against the applicable official recall notice, including:

- exact product name and formulation;
- manufacturer or labeler;
- NDC, if present;
- lot number as printed on the bottle;
- expiration date;
- whether the notice covers a lot range or an all-lot distribution;
- packaging or labeling details and purchase/distribution information.

The FDA result itself labels these as candidate product matches—not bottle-level clearance—and states that a nonmatching lot does not prove the product was unaffected. The list is also bounded and may include historical records; enforcement records can lag announcements and may omit consumer instructions. Source: [FDA OpenFDA enforcement query](https://api.fda.gov/drug/enforcement.json?search=product_description%3A%22naproxen%22&sort=report_date%3Adesc&limit=20).
